systemic, I think when someone
says systemic racism to me,

00:29:12.484 --> 00:29:14.552
that means that there are laws

00:29:14.552 --> 00:29:16.855
that are in place
that are racist.

00:29:16.855 --> 00:29:19.357
So as far as I know
there are no laws

00:29:19.357 --> 00:29:20.825
that are in place
that are racist.

00:29:20.825 --> 00:29:23.228 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think you're talking about
the sort of second order

00:29:23.228 --> 00:29:25.797 align:start size:94% position:6%
of there may be more
policing in certain areas

00:29:25.797 --> 00:29:27.532
because there's more crime.

00:29:27.532 --> 00:29:29.801 align:start size:97% position:3%
So then and then I think what
a lot of people would say

00:29:29.801 --> 00:29:31.736
well, that has more to
do with the breakdown

00:29:31.736 --> 00:29:33.872 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the black family
and why that happened.

00:29:33.872 --> 00:29:35.473
Something like that.

00:29:35.473 --> 00:29:37.809
- Right, well, first of all,

00:29:37.809 --> 00:29:41.646
to me systemic can
still be part of the,

00:29:41.646 --> 00:29:45.250 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know the, an
organization, the environment.

00:29:45.250 --> 00:29:46.084
So

00:29:46.985 --> 00:29:47.786
if

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:50.355
cops are

00:29:50.355 --> 00:29:54.092
out there arresting people
of color at a higher rate,

00:29:54.092 --> 00:29:57.162 align:start size:94% position:6%
because that's either
what they're rewarded for,

00:29:57.162 --> 00:29:58.730
or that's the hidden message

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:00.431
or they're just outright told

00:30:00.431 --> 00:30:03.635 align:start size:94% position:6%
you need to go out to this
neighborhood more often

00:30:03.635 --> 00:30:06.070
then that's still systemic,
even if it's not written.

00:30:06.070 --> 00:30:10.341
But like I said, there are
written laws for penalties

00:30:10.341 --> 00:30:13.778
so that drugs that
people of color use

00:30:13.778 --> 00:30:15.613
have longer prison sentences.

00:30:15.613 --> 00:30:17.482
So that's actually in the law.

00:30:17.482 --> 00:30:19.184 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, do you know
of any of those

00:30:19.184 --> 00:30:20.418
that are still on the books?

00:30:20.418 --> 00:30:21.719
I mean, I know that
in New York city,

00:30:21.719 --> 00:30:23.755
they used to have
the law about crack

00:30:23.755 --> 00:30:26.624 align:start size:84% position:16%
got a higher
sentence than cocaine

00:30:26.624 --> 00:30:28.159
and it was generally thought

00:30:28.159 --> 00:30:30.028
that more black people use
crack 'cause it was easy.

00:30:30.028 --> 00:30:31.529
It was cheaper to get

00:30:31.529 --> 00:30:33.264 line:0%
but I know New York
city got rid of that.

00:30:33.264 --> 00:30:35.233 line:0%
Are there still laws like that?

00:30:35.233 --> 00:30:38.369 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- Oh yeah and in
fact I did check with

00:30:38.369 --> 00:30:42.006
one of my respected friends
at Cato to just check in,

00:30:42.006 --> 00:30:44.042
I think about three,

00:30:44.042 --> 00:30:45.944 align:start size:88% position:13%
yeah, maybe about three
or four weeks ago.

00:30:45.944 --> 00:30:47.579 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I said, okay,
just wanna make sure

00:30:47.579 --> 00:30:49.113 align:start size:84% position:16%
that all of these
laws we were talking

00:30:49.113 --> 00:30:51.850 align:start size:84% position:16%
about before with
the prison sentences.

00:30:51.850 --> 00:30:54.352
And so and he said, yeah,
so I didn't go into detail

00:30:54.352 --> 00:30:56.020
just because there
were so many of them

00:30:56.020 --> 00:30:59.490
and yes, crack cocaine is
one example then crack.

00:30:59.490 --> 00:31:01.793
And of course now we
get into the drug war

00:31:01.793 --> 00:31:04.429
and I can't, I have
to at least point out

00:31:04.429 --> 00:31:07.799 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Milton Friedman told
us that crack cocaine

00:31:07.799 --> 00:31:09.767
would have never been invented

00:31:09.767 --> 00:31:11.936
had cocaine not been legal.

00:31:11.936 --> 00:31:15.106
It's only because
cocaine was illegal

00:31:15.106 --> 00:31:17.342
that crack cocaine
was even invented

00:31:17.342 --> 00:31:19.210
just like during prohibition,

00:31:20.078 --> 00:31:21.746
people drank less wine and beer

00:31:21.746 --> 00:31:24.115 align:start position:0%
and then went to liquor because
it was easier to smuggle

00:31:24.115 --> 00:31:25.316
because it was more powerful.

00:31:25.316 --> 00:31:26.784
So

00:31:26.784 --> 00:31:29.087
and so I've mentioned
the racist war on drugs

00:31:29.087 --> 00:31:31.656
and people wondered
what I mean by that.

00:31:31.656 --> 00:31:34.325 align:start size:88% position:13%
Again, we go right back
to the war on drugs

00:31:34.325 --> 00:31:36.060
is hurting everybody, of course.

00:31:37.729 --> 00:31:42.433 align:start size:97% position:3%
But, especially with the
uneven prison sentences, yes.

00:31:42.433 --> 00:31:43.868
- Yeah, so it's interesting

00:31:43.868 --> 00:31:45.670
because I think what it
sort of whittles down,

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:47.372
I think a lot of my
audience is gonna say

00:31:47.372 --> 00:31:50.575 align:start size:88% position:13%
the way you're defining
systemic racism

00:31:50.575 --> 00:31:54.245 align:start size:94% position:6%
is a little different than
the way they define it

00:31:54.245 --> 00:31:56.247 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is why I
asked that question.

00:31:56.247 --> 00:31:58.116 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because there
aren't specific laws

00:31:58.116 --> 00:32:00.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
or if there was a
specific police department

00:32:00.752 --> 00:32:03.488 align:start size:97% position:3%
that said arrest more, you
know, arrest more black people

00:32:03.488 --> 00:32:06.891
that's in our code of conduct
then of course that is wrong.

00:32:06.891 --> 00:32:08.126
But if the question is,

00:32:08.126 --> 00:32:10.395
Oh, there happens
to be more policing

00:32:10.395 --> 00:32:12.230
in an area that has more crime

00:32:12.230 --> 00:32:14.499
and there happen to be
more black people there,

00:32:14.499 --> 00:32:16.267
well then we have to
figure out why that is.

00:32:16.267 --> 00:32:18.503
I think most people
bring that again

00:32:18.503 --> 00:32:21.806
to the welfare state and
the breakdown of the family

00:32:21.806 --> 00:32:24.142
and the welfare state is
very much a lefty policy.

00:32:24.142 --> 00:32:26.344 align:start size:84% position:16%
Certainly not a
libertarian policy.

00:32:27.211 --> 00:32:28.413
- Right, absolutely.

00:32:28.413 --> 00:32:30.315
But, well, let me just say that,

00:32:30.315 --> 00:32:34.218 align:start size:88% position:13%
I agree with your
listeners, your viewers,

00:32:34.218 --> 00:32:37.655
that yes, the free
market can handle

00:32:39.090 --> 00:32:41.793
these problems that we've
had with discrimination

00:32:41.793 --> 00:32:43.328
much better than the government.

00:32:43.328 --> 00:32:45.730
And that now what we have is

00:32:45.730 --> 00:32:47.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
we're discriminating
against Asians

00:32:47.832 --> 00:32:51.769
trying to get into Harvard
and go right down the line.

00:32:51.769 --> 00:32:56.741
It's gotten completely out of
hand and I completely agree.

00:32:57.976 --> 00:33:00.178
- Yeah, so on the
drug part of this,

00:33:00.178 --> 00:33:01.779 align:start size:84% position:16%
I find this to be a
really interesting one

00:33:01.779 --> 00:33:03.781
because, you know, I
always describe myself

00:33:03.781 --> 00:33:05.616
as a classical liberal
and people always say,

00:33:05.616 --> 00:33:07.018
well, Dave you sound
like a libertarian,

00:33:07.018 --> 00:33:07.986
what is a classical liberal?

00:33:07.986 --> 00:33:09.454
And I always say, well, look,

00:33:09.454 --> 00:33:12.657 align:start size:94% position:6%
a classical liberal in
effect is what I would say

00:33:12.657 --> 00:33:15.526 align:start size:84% position:16%
a really sort of a
realist libertarian is

00:33:15.526 --> 00:33:17.895
meaning that I love all
the libertarian ideas

00:33:17.895 --> 00:33:20.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I understand I
can't win all the time

00:33:20.131 --> 00:33:23.401
and I'm gonna have to put
some guard rails there.

00:33:23.401 --> 00:33:26.404 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's the most simplest way
that I would describe it.

00:33:26.404 --> 00:33:28.439
So in my book, I talk
about the drug war

00:33:28.439 --> 00:33:30.908 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I'm for
legalizing marijuana.

00:33:30.908 --> 00:33:33.478
I'm certainly for legalizing

00:33:33.478 --> 00:33:34.846
psychedelics

00:33:34.846 --> 00:33:37.949
and then we can whittle
down every little drug,

00:33:37.949 --> 00:33:41.519 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I'm not personally
for legalizing

00:33:41.519 --> 00:33:43.287
the seriously hardcore stuff.

00:33:43.287 --> 00:33:45.356
And it's not because I
wanna put people in jail

00:33:45.356 --> 00:33:46.824
because of their decisions.

00:33:46.824 --> 00:33:50.228
But the simple truth is
I know that if someone

00:33:50.228 --> 00:33:52.497
was cooking meth
next door to me,

00:33:52.497 --> 00:33:54.832
I know that there's
gonna be more crime.

00:33:54.832 --> 00:33:58.102
And I know that it's going
to be riskier to be out there

00:33:58.102 --> 00:34:00.371
and you don't want kids
there and the rest of it.

00:34:00.371 --> 00:34:03.674 align:start size:94% position:6%
So are you for legalizing
absolutely everything

00:34:03.674 --> 00:34:05.877
with no regulations
or is this a place

00:34:05.877 --> 00:34:08.613
where you you find
some middle ground,

00:34:08.613 --> 00:34:10.748
I know you would kick it
to the States regardless,

00:34:10.748 --> 00:34:12.450
but where do you fall on this?

00:34:12.450 --> 00:34:15.153
- Yeah, I mean, yeah,
the States right now

00:34:15.153 --> 00:34:17.355
have laws on drinking

00:34:17.355 --> 00:34:20.658
and I was appalled when
I moved from a state that

00:34:20.658 --> 00:34:22.627 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, you could drink
pretty much anytime you want

00:34:22.627 --> 00:34:23.961
and then I moved to the South

00:34:23.961 --> 00:34:25.663
and I was shocked
when I found out,

00:34:25.663 --> 00:34:27.999
what, you can't buy
alcohol on Sunday.

00:34:27.999 --> 00:34:30.001
You know, what kind of,
why did I end up here?

00:34:30.001 --> 00:34:32.637 align:start size:88% position:13%
And in fact, we couldn't
buy alcohol on Sunday

00:34:32.637 --> 00:34:33.938
until pretty recently.

00:34:35.306 --> 00:34:39.310 align:start size:97% position:3%
So you know, yes, even though
I don't like those laws,

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:43.114
at least there's some
competition among laws,

00:34:43.114 --> 00:34:45.316 align:start size:94% position:6%
unless you get the federal
government involved.

00:34:45.316 --> 00:34:49.187 align:start size:94% position:6%
And by the way I realized
I'm gonna kind of

00:34:49.187 --> 00:34:50.822 align:start size:81% position:19%
leave the topic
for just a minute,

00:34:50.822 --> 00:34:52.523
just to explain real quick

00:34:52.523 --> 00:34:54.292 align:start size:88% position:13%
because a lot of
people don't know that,

00:34:54.292 --> 00:34:57.862 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, we had,
when I was in school,

00:34:57.862 --> 00:34:59.697 align:start size:84% position:16%
in fact, I was a
bartender in college

00:34:59.697 --> 00:35:00.965
because I was in a state

00:35:00.965 --> 00:35:02.767
where the drinking
age was on the 18.

00:35:02.767 --> 00:35:04.435
And that's the way
it was back then.

00:35:04.435 --> 00:35:06.971
The different States had
different drinking ages

00:35:06.971 --> 00:35:11.776 align:start size:84% position:16%
until Elizabeth
Dole Transportation
Secretary in the 90's

00:35:11.776 --> 00:35:13.878
came along, took in, you know,

00:35:13.878 --> 00:35:15.146 align:start size:84% position:16%
just like government
always does,

00:35:15.146 --> 00:35:15.980
federal government always does

00:35:15.980 --> 00:35:17.448
takes in the money

00:35:17.448 --> 00:35:19.884 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then dangles the highway
funds in front of States

00:35:19.884 --> 00:35:23.454
and says, hey, you want
your highway money back?

00:35:23.454 --> 00:35:25.089
You're gonna have to
raise the drinking age.

00:35:25.089 --> 00:35:26.524
Now we can have it as bait.

00:35:26.524 --> 00:35:29.360 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, maybe 21 is a
better drinking age, whatever.

00:35:29.360 --> 00:35:30.862
But the point is,

00:35:30.862 --> 00:35:33.231
it shouldn't be the federal
government deciding that,

00:35:33.231 --> 00:35:35.032
it should be the States.

00:35:35.032 --> 00:35:38.136
So when I say, okay,
let's have the States

00:35:38.136 --> 00:35:42.173 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, we'll let the States
regulate it if they like,

00:35:42.173 --> 00:35:44.675
I mean, really let
them regulate it

00:35:44.675 --> 00:35:46.611
not have the federal
government, you know,

00:35:46.611 --> 00:35:50.148
backdoor in there trying to
hold bribes over their heads

00:35:50.148 --> 00:35:53.017
so they can do what they want.

00:35:53.017 --> 00:35:54.185
So really...

00:35:54.185 --> 00:35:57.054
- So you'd let them do it?

00:35:57.054 --> 00:35:59.290 align:start size:84% position:16%
- (indistinct)
confused what you said

00:35:59.290 --> 00:36:01.926 align:start size:84% position:16%
because you said that
you wouldn't legalize
the harder drugs

00:36:01.926 --> 00:36:03.327
but then you talked about,

00:36:03.327 --> 00:36:06.764 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, having somebody
having a meth lab,

00:36:06.764 --> 00:36:08.933 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, I guess is a
meth lab, a harder drug?

00:36:08.933 --> 00:36:10.168
I'm confused.

00:36:10.168 --> 00:36:12.370 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, well, meaning
I wouldn't want

00:36:12.370 --> 00:36:15.373
if meth was totally legal,

00:36:15.373 --> 00:36:19.110 align:start size:97% position:3%
then I suspect that more
meth labs would be popping up

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:22.079
and I know that right now,
if someone moved in next door

00:36:22.079 --> 00:36:23.848
and was cooking meth next to me,

00:36:23.848 --> 00:36:25.850
while we know it's it's illegal

00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:28.219 align:start size:94% position:6%
and someone will hopefully
do something about it,

00:36:28.219 --> 00:36:29.687
assuming we still have
a police department here

00:36:29.687 --> 00:36:31.589
in Los Angeles, I'm not sure.

00:36:31.589 --> 00:36:33.791 align:start size:94% position:6%
but if it was legal,
there would be no recourse

00:36:33.791 --> 00:36:34.892
and we know what will happen.

00:36:34.892 --> 00:36:36.127
Crime will expand,

00:36:36.127 --> 00:36:37.361
you'll get shady
or people around,

00:36:37.361 --> 00:36:40.298
your property value
will go down, etc.

00:36:40.298 --> 00:36:41.933 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well I see it
completely differently

00:36:41.933 --> 00:36:43.768
because we've got alcohol.

00:36:43.768 --> 00:36:46.671
Well, alcohol is legal
and I just don't hear

00:36:46.671 --> 00:36:48.973 align:start size:84% position:16%
of many people
making bathtub gin,

00:36:48.973 --> 00:36:50.408
but that's part of the problem

00:36:50.408 --> 00:36:53.010 align:start size:97% position:3%
is when you legalize
something or decriminalize it

00:36:53.010 --> 00:36:56.614
however you wanna do it is
it has to truly be legal.

00:36:56.614 --> 00:36:59.784 align:start position:0%
So for instance, in California,
they've legalized marijuana

00:36:59.784 --> 00:37:02.420 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they've limited the
number of people can sell it.

00:37:02.420 --> 00:37:03.821 align:start size:84% position:16%
They've got all
these licensing laws

00:37:03.821 --> 00:37:05.189
you've got to pay
all these fees.

00:37:05.189 --> 00:37:08.926
So there's still an
underground market.

00:37:08.926 --> 00:37:11.862 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's still people
selling marijuana illegally.

00:37:11.862 --> 00:37:14.599
So that means the
laws aren't right.

00:37:14.599 --> 00:37:16.267
Again, when's the
last time you heard

00:37:16.267 --> 00:37:18.002
of anybody selling bathtub gin

00:37:18.002 --> 00:37:19.904 align:start size:84% position:16%
that would cause
somebody to go blind?

00:37:19.904 --> 00:37:21.839
You don't because it's legal

00:37:21.839 --> 00:37:24.675 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the point that we go
down and we buy it safely.

00:37:24.675 --> 00:37:26.978
So if meth were legal

00:37:26.978 --> 00:37:28.813 align:start size:88% position:13%
there wouldn't be people
making it next door.

00:37:28.813 --> 00:37:30.581
They'd be making it
down at Philip Morris

00:37:30.581 --> 00:37:32.683
or whoever, Seagram's, you know,

00:37:32.683 --> 00:37:34.452
whoever makes the other drugs.

00:37:35.620 --> 00:37:37.555 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Okay, so I'm not
with you there,

00:37:37.555 --> 00:37:39.690
but I do understand
the line of thinking.

00:37:39.690 --> 00:37:40.791
That's just fine.

00:37:40.791 --> 00:37:41.726
But what would you say?

00:37:41.726 --> 00:37:43.261
This is where the conservatives

00:37:43.261 --> 00:37:46.264
I think see the libertarian
movement as sort of bananas.

00:37:46.264 --> 00:37:47.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
'Cause I think this
is where conservatives

00:37:47.832 --> 00:37:50.167
would say, okay, I get freedom.

00:37:50.167 --> 00:37:52.837
I get States rights,
I get all that stuff.

00:37:52.837 --> 00:37:55.039
But you're telling
me that in effect,

00:37:55.039 --> 00:37:57.808
if there was enough
demand for meth,

00:37:57.808 --> 00:38:01.846 align:start position:0%
that Philip Morris and Unilever
should start making meth

00:38:01.846 --> 00:38:03.314
if the market demands it

00:38:03.314 --> 00:38:06.217 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we know that a society
filled with meth is not great.

00:38:06.217 --> 00:38:08.152
So the conservatives
would take sort of,

00:38:08.152 --> 00:38:12.056
we want to conserve sort
of a functioning society

00:38:12.056 --> 00:38:13.557 align:start size:84% position:16%
where the libertarians
are saying

00:38:13.557 --> 00:38:15.526
it's more of a free for all.

00:38:15.526 --> 00:38:17.428
Do you think that's like a fair,

00:38:17.428 --> 00:38:20.398 align:start position:0%
it's just sort of like a
philosophically different spot?

00:38:21.999 --> 00:38:24.302 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Well, I think
they're misguided.

00:38:24.302 --> 00:38:28.005
First off, if you look
where marijuana is legal

00:38:28.005 --> 00:38:31.409 align:start size:94% position:6%
you haven't seen increase
in marijuana use,

00:38:31.409 --> 00:38:32.710
let's say in teenagers

00:38:32.710 --> 00:38:36.614
and you haven't seen
it basically abused.

00:38:36.614 --> 00:38:39.350
You know, again, we
look at alcohol...

00:38:39.350 --> 00:38:42.253
- But marijuana is
not highly addictive

00:38:42.253 --> 00:38:44.455
the way meth or, you
know some opiates,

00:38:44.455 --> 00:38:46.757
you know, some of the
more hardcore stuff is.

00:38:46.757 --> 00:38:48.659
- Well, alcohol is
highly addictive.

00:38:48.659 --> 00:38:50.127
Tobacco's highly addictive.

00:38:50.127 --> 00:38:51.562
Those are very highly active.

00:38:51.562 --> 00:38:54.632 align:start size:97% position:3%
In fact, our own surgeon
general said that tobacco was,

00:38:54.632 --> 00:38:56.667
the former surgeon general,

00:38:56.667 --> 00:38:58.636 align:start size:94% position:6%
I believe it was C.
Everett Koop who said that

00:38:58.636 --> 00:39:02.306 align:start size:97% position:3%
tobacco was just as a
highly addictive as heroin.

00:39:02.306 --> 00:39:03.541
So

00:39:03.541 --> 00:39:06.310 align:start size:84% position:16%
and yet that's
legal, but you know,

00:39:06.310 --> 00:39:10.247 align:start size:97% position:3%
what we have right now is
we've got drugs being illegal

00:39:10.247 --> 00:39:12.350
and so they don't go for help

00:39:12.350 --> 00:39:14.485
and even look at where
it starts, you know,

00:39:14.485 --> 00:39:16.354
again, when was the
last time you heard

00:39:16.354 --> 00:39:18.155 align:start size:88% position:13%
of a liquor store
owner going up and down

00:39:18.155 --> 00:39:20.491 align:start size:97% position:3%
the halls of a high
school, trying to sell gin?

00:39:20.491 --> 00:39:22.727
If it's legal and the
profits aren't there.

00:39:22.727 --> 00:39:24.729 align:start size:84% position:16%
You're not gonna have
the profit motive

00:39:24.729 --> 00:39:26.597
for people to go
out and sell it.

00:39:26.597 --> 00:39:27.698
Or

00:39:27.698 --> 00:39:29.467
I ask when's the last time

00:39:29.467 --> 00:39:31.068
you ever heard of a vodka addict

00:39:32.470 --> 00:39:34.872 align:start size:97% position:3%
breaking into houses in order
to support a vodka habit?

00:39:34.872 --> 00:39:36.273
You know, you can
make enough money

00:39:36.273 --> 00:39:38.476
to support it if it's legal.

00:39:38.476 --> 00:39:39.977
When things are illegal

00:39:39.977 --> 00:39:42.313
they tend to cost 20
to 25 times higher.

00:39:42.313 --> 00:39:44.949
So that's 20 to 25
times as many houses

00:39:44.949 --> 00:39:46.183
as you have to break into

00:39:46.183 --> 00:39:47.885
and so I'm looking at it

00:39:47.885 --> 00:39:49.653
and by the way, I'm
not a drug user.

00:39:49.653 --> 00:39:51.255
Well, actually I am.

00:39:51.255 --> 00:39:52.757
Bourbon is my drug of choice.

00:39:52.757 --> 00:39:54.125
(Dave laughing)

00:39:54.125 --> 00:39:55.860
Yes, bourbon is a drug

00:39:55.860 --> 00:39:58.462
and I got a graduate certificate

00:39:58.462 --> 00:40:00.297
in Drug and Alcohol Studies

00:40:00.297 --> 00:40:03.801
and I understand that the way
our neuro-transmitters work

00:40:03.801 --> 00:40:05.469
alcohol is actually a
very dangerous drug.

00:40:05.469 --> 00:40:07.938 align:start size:84% position:16%
Much more dangerous
than marijuana.

00:40:07.938 --> 00:40:11.275
But somehow I got
through the 1970s

00:40:11.275 --> 00:40:13.444
in high school and college
never having tried marijuana.

00:40:13.444 --> 00:40:15.946 align:start size:88% position:13%
I gotta admit, you know,
I'm a goody two shoes

00:40:15.946 --> 00:40:17.381
but

00:40:17.381 --> 00:40:21.051 align:start size:94% position:6%
the point here is there's
not a profit motive.

00:40:21.051 --> 00:40:25.089
You know when people ask
me about legalizing drugs

00:40:25.089 --> 00:40:26.857
the point is and they say,

00:40:26.857 --> 00:40:28.959
well, but I don't use
drugs so I don't care.

00:40:28.959 --> 00:40:32.029
I say, but you should care
if you care about crime.

00:40:32.029 --> 00:40:34.832
You should care if your
kids are in a school

00:40:34.832 --> 00:40:37.368 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause right now
there's a profit motive

00:40:37.368 --> 00:40:39.670
to get your kids hooked on drugs

00:40:39.670 --> 00:40:41.806
and high school kids
themselves will say,

00:40:41.806 --> 00:40:44.275
you know, it's a lot
easier to buy marijuana

00:40:44.275 --> 00:40:45.943
than it is to buy alcohol.

00:40:45.943 --> 00:40:47.378
Yeah, 'cause alcohol is legal,

00:40:47.378 --> 00:40:48.846
there's not the profit in there.

00:40:48.846 --> 00:40:50.347
There's competition.

00:40:50.347 --> 00:40:52.550
It's, we've got the free market
out there taking care of it.

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:54.185
So how about let's
have the free market

00:40:54.185 --> 00:40:55.419
take care of the other things

00:40:55.419 --> 00:40:57.221 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then if you do
have a heroin problem,

00:40:57.221 --> 00:41:00.825 align:start size:84% position:16%
let's treat it as a
medical issue and not,

00:41:02.026 --> 00:41:03.360
you know, a criminal issue but,

00:41:03.360 --> 00:41:05.429 align:start size:84% position:16%
and also I'm confused
because you said,

00:41:05.429 --> 00:41:08.098 align:start size:88% position:13%
well, I'm not, you
want it to remain legal

00:41:08.098 --> 00:41:09.533
but you're not for
locking people up.

00:41:09.533 --> 00:41:11.302 align:start size:81% position:19%
But isn't that
the definition of,

00:41:11.302 --> 00:41:14.071
if it's illegal then you do
lock people up, I'm confused.

00:41:14.071 --> 00:41:16.540
- Well, no, I'm with you
on the last part of that,

00:41:16.540 --> 00:41:19.877 align:start size:97% position:3%
in that if someone was
addicted to one of the drugs

00:41:19.877 --> 00:41:21.412
that I think should be illegal,

00:41:21.412 --> 00:41:24.482
say meth or opiates or
something like that,

00:41:24.482 --> 00:41:26.417
I wouldn't wanna send
them to jail for that.

00:41:26.417 --> 00:41:27.618
I would wanna get them help.

00:41:27.618 --> 00:41:29.253 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't think putting
someone in jail

00:41:29.253 --> 00:41:31.055
(indistinct)

00:41:31.055 --> 00:41:32.490
Sorry?

00:41:32.490 --> 00:41:34.458 align:start size:97% position:3%
- But isn't that what
illegal means that if you...

00:41:34.458 --> 00:41:37.461
- Well, when I say illegal,

00:41:37.461 --> 00:41:39.296
I was sort of giving
you shorthand on that,

00:41:39.296 --> 00:41:40.865
but when I say illegal I mean,

00:41:40.865 --> 00:41:42.533
if you're selling it

00:41:42.533 --> 00:41:45.236
then there should be
a repercussion for it.

00:41:45.236 --> 00:41:46.770
A legal repercussion.

00:41:46.770 --> 00:41:50.274
If you're just using it or if
you're mass distributing it

00:41:50.274 --> 00:41:54.078
I see that different as
someone that is using it.

00:41:54.078 --> 00:41:56.080 align:start size:84% position:16%
And again, this is
just one of those ones

00:41:56.080 --> 00:41:58.249
where I find that sort of the,

00:41:58.249 --> 00:42:00.251 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's what we're
basically agreeing here

00:42:00.251 --> 00:42:01.719 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then there's like
this little space

00:42:01.719 --> 00:42:03.587
where it's like
okay, how do you,

00:42:03.587 --> 00:42:05.923 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's a more, quite a little
more of a liberal approach,

00:42:05.923 --> 00:42:07.291
a little more of a
conservative approach

00:42:07.291 --> 00:42:09.727
and a libertarian approach.

00:42:09.727 --> 00:42:11.028
And you can remain a good person

00:42:11.028 --> 00:42:12.062
and not totally agree on it.

00:42:12.062 --> 00:42:13.464
Isn't that crazy?

00:42:13.464 --> 00:42:14.698
- Yeah and by the way,

00:42:14.698 --> 00:42:16.634
you can think that
every drug should be

00:42:17.768 --> 00:42:19.203 align:start size:84% position:16%
legal even though
you've never used them

00:42:19.203 --> 00:42:21.138 align:start size:81% position:19%
and Bourbon's your
drug of choice

00:42:21.972 --> 00:42:23.173
so again...

00:42:23.173 --> 00:42:25.809
- I'm more of a red
wine guy myself.

00:42:25.809 --> 00:42:27.611
- Oh, well, you know what, I
do have red wine with dinner.

00:42:27.611 --> 00:42:31.649
Yes, if you're a red
wine guy, then good

00:42:31.649 --> 00:42:34.985
we're on the same boat.

00:42:34.985 --> 00:42:36.620
- Yeah, all right, let's
just keep going through

00:42:36.620 --> 00:42:38.556
just sort of the biggest hits

00:42:38.556 --> 00:42:39.857
and sort of the big issues.

00:42:39.857 --> 00:42:42.560 align:start size:81% position:19%
So foreign policy,
it seems to me

00:42:43.994 --> 00:42:47.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Trump has been pretty
solid on foreign policy

00:42:47.531 --> 00:42:50.434
in that it doesn't seem like
we're going to extra wars.

00:42:50.434 --> 00:42:51.969
We've got a couple
of peace agreements

00:42:51.969 --> 00:42:54.438
that have been signed in
the last couple of weeks.

00:42:54.438 --> 00:42:56.640
He's trying to tell the people,

00:42:56.640 --> 00:42:58.609
we shouldn't be in
Afghanistan anymore.

00:42:58.609 --> 00:43:00.844
We got some troops out of Iraq.

00:43:00.844 --> 00:43:03.314
It doesn't look like we're
gonna be nation building

00:43:03.314 --> 00:43:04.982
anytime soon.

00:43:04.982 --> 00:43:07.718 align:start size:88% position:13%
Do you see those things
as wins and sort of

00:43:07.718 --> 00:43:10.888 align:start position:0%
what would a purely libertarian
foreign policy look like?

00:43:12.323 --> 00:43:16.026
- Yeah, we're in about 160
countries around the world.

00:43:16.026 --> 00:43:19.997 align:start position:0%
I think we were in 160
countries when Trump took office

00:43:19.997 --> 00:43:22.766 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I think we're
still in 160 countries.

00:43:22.766 --> 00:43:24.702
So if you wanna say
we've made progress

00:43:24.702 --> 00:43:27.004
because now we're
in 159 countries.

00:43:27.004 --> 00:43:28.839 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, I don't see
that as much progress.

00:43:28.839 --> 00:43:31.675
And we spend more on
military than the next

00:43:31.675 --> 00:43:33.944 align:start size:84% position:16%
probably eight
countries combined.

00:43:33.944 --> 00:43:37.414 align:start size:94% position:6%
So and it's making us
less safe, not more safe.

00:43:37.414 --> 00:43:38.849
That's the worst part about it.

00:43:38.849 --> 00:43:41.785 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, no, we need to seriously
bring the troops home

00:43:41.785 --> 00:43:43.420
and if I were president

00:43:43.420 --> 00:43:46.290
I would turn America into
one giant Switzerland,

00:43:46.290 --> 00:43:49.994 align:start size:94% position:6%
armed and neutral in which
we protect our borders.

00:43:49.994 --> 00:43:51.261
Yes, we should have borders.

00:43:51.261 --> 00:43:53.263
We need to protect them.

00:43:53.263 --> 00:43:55.332
However we shouldn't be
protecting the borders

00:43:55.332 --> 00:43:56.800
of everybody else.

00:43:56.800 --> 00:43:59.570 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I've pointed out, of
course, half facetiously here.

00:43:59.570 --> 00:44:01.438
We keep hearing how
isn't France great?

00:44:01.438 --> 00:44:02.740
They get five week vacations.

00:44:02.740 --> 00:44:04.508
We should get five
weeks vacations.

00:44:04.508 --> 00:44:06.610 align:start position:0%
Well, yeah, maybe if we weren't
paying for their military

00:44:06.610 --> 00:44:09.013 align:start size:84% position:16%
we could get five
weeks vacations too.

00:44:09.013 --> 00:44:11.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
Why are we paying for
everybody else's military

00:44:11.315 --> 00:44:12.249
around the world?

00:44:12.249 --> 00:44:14.084
Why should a taxpayer in Alabama

00:44:14.084 --> 00:44:16.320 align:start size:88% position:13%
be paying for, you
know, Frances military?

00:44:16.320 --> 00:44:17.554
That's absurd.

00:44:17.554 --> 00:44:19.823
And I was actually
excited when I heard

00:44:19.823 --> 00:44:21.058
that he was bringing,

00:44:21.058 --> 00:44:22.893
that Trump was bringing
troops out of Germany.

00:44:22.893 --> 00:44:24.161
I thought, oh finally,

00:44:24.161 --> 00:44:26.463
a small step, but step
in the right direction

00:44:26.463 --> 00:44:28.799
and then I heard he was
putting them in Poland.

00:44:28.799 --> 00:44:30.200
No, he needs to keep going.

00:44:30.200 --> 00:44:31.568
They need to come all way home.

00:44:31.568 --> 00:44:33.771 line:0%
They need to come to
the United States.

00:44:33.771 --> 00:44:35.506 line:0%
Again, one giant Switzerland.

00:44:35.506 --> 00:44:37.808 line:0%
We need to protect our borders.

00:44:37.808 --> 00:44:39.276 line:0%
- Yeah, so it's interesting

00:44:39.276 --> 00:44:41.078 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you've got
obviously some differences

00:44:41.078 --> 00:44:42.880 align:start size:94% position:6%
with Trump there, but
when he says things like,

00:44:42.880 --> 00:44:45.416
you know, you gotta pay
your share to be a NATO,

00:44:45.416 --> 00:44:48.252
you fundamentally agree with
that sort of thing, right?

00:44:48.252 --> 00:44:49.787
That the US should not, well...

00:44:50.988 --> 00:44:52.056
- I don't think we
should be in NATO.

00:44:52.056 --> 00:44:53.323
I thought he didn't like NATO.

00:44:53.323 --> 00:44:54.792 align:start size:84% position:16%
I thought he was
gonna get out of NATO.

00:44:54.792 --> 00:44:56.293
Isn't that what he was saying?

00:44:57.594 --> 00:44:59.163
- I think, well, I think
he was at least saying

00:44:59.163 --> 00:45:01.165 align:start size:97% position:3%
that the other countries
have to pay their fair share

00:45:01.165 --> 00:45:02.666
or we'd get out and I guess we,

00:45:02.666 --> 00:45:04.268
I guess we did at
least cut funding.

00:45:04.268 --> 00:45:06.870
I don't know if anyone
made up that funding.

00:45:06.870 --> 00:45:09.339
- Well, I got a great way
for the other countries

00:45:09.339 --> 00:45:10.808
to pay their fair share.

00:45:10.808 --> 00:45:13.077
We get out completely and we
don't pay anybody's fair share.

00:45:13.077 --> 00:45:14.445
How's that?

00:45:14.445 --> 00:45:15.446
- [Dave] Yeah.

00:45:15.446 --> 00:45:16.747
- Problem solved.

00:45:16.747 --> 00:45:20.217
- So if we were to have,
you know, strong borders

00:45:20.217 --> 00:45:24.054
and a military to protect
the United States itself

00:45:24.054 --> 00:45:26.423
do you think we have
any responsibility,

00:45:26.423 --> 00:45:28.292
or are there any situations

00:45:28.292 --> 00:45:31.528 align:start position:0%
where there could be a genocide
anywhere else in the world,

00:45:31.528 --> 00:45:35.432
or you know, an evil
dictatorial regime

00:45:35.432 --> 00:45:36.967
or whatever it might be,

00:45:36.967 --> 00:45:40.738
would you see any situation
where we should get involved

00:45:40.738 --> 00:45:43.040
to help people that
are not Americans?

00:45:44.374 --> 00:45:47.444 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Not from the standpoint
of the government.

00:45:47.444 --> 00:45:49.880
Now we had George
Clooney, you know

00:45:49.880 --> 00:45:53.717
trying to help people
in war torn Sudan.

00:45:53.717 --> 00:45:56.019
We've had, or Syria, I'm sorry.

00:45:56.019 --> 00:45:58.222 align:start size:84% position:16%
We've heard of other
people going over

00:45:58.222 --> 00:46:01.125 align:start size:97% position:3%
and try to make humanitarian
relief missions.

00:46:01.125 --> 00:46:03.460
You know, Americans
are very generous.

00:46:03.460 --> 00:46:04.661
They're very helpful.

00:46:04.661 --> 00:46:06.263 align:start size:84% position:16%
They want to go over
and help people.

00:46:06.263 --> 00:46:09.066
So let's let them help people.

00:46:09.066 --> 00:46:11.301
But as far as the US military,

00:46:11.301 --> 00:46:14.204
as far as going in,

00:46:14.204 --> 00:46:17.508 align:start size:88% position:13%
anytime we go over there
to do something good,

00:46:17.508 --> 00:46:20.177
something bad, always happens.

00:46:20.177 --> 00:46:21.545
There's always,

00:46:21.545 --> 00:46:24.281
you know, they call them
unintended consequences.

00:46:24.281 --> 00:46:27.251
Maybe they're unintended
but they're not unforeseen

00:46:27.251 --> 00:46:28.685
because anytime you go in

00:46:28.685 --> 00:46:30.687
and you pick one
side as the winner,

00:46:30.687 --> 00:46:31.922
there's going to be a loser,

00:46:31.922 --> 00:46:33.757
who's not going to be
very happy with us.

00:46:33.757 --> 00:46:36.593
So I would leave it up
to the very generous

00:46:36.593 --> 00:46:38.862
and very fair minded Americans

00:46:38.862 --> 00:46:40.764
to do whatever they
like on their own

00:46:40.764 --> 00:46:43.200
and keep the military out of it.

00:46:43.200 --> 00:46:45.869 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, is there an
inherent problem there though

00:46:45.869 --> 00:46:47.504
that if this was World War II,

00:46:47.504 --> 00:46:49.573
that the United States
would just be sitting

00:46:49.573 --> 00:46:52.309
on the sidelines
and that, you know,

00:46:52.309 --> 00:46:54.111
the Nazis could have
taken over Europe?

00:46:54.111 --> 00:46:55.579
I get, we're not Europe.

00:46:55.579 --> 00:46:58.215 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, do you see a moral
quandary there at all?

00:46:59.349 --> 00:47:00.584 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, I'm glad
you brought that up.

00:47:00.584 --> 00:47:02.152 align:start size:88% position:13%
Actually many
libertarians think that

00:47:02.152 --> 00:47:04.521 align:start size:84% position:16%
we shouldn't have
gotten into the war,

00:47:04.521 --> 00:47:05.989
but I'd like to point out

00:47:05.989 --> 00:47:10.494
that we were brought into World
War II when Japan bombed us.

00:47:10.494 --> 00:47:12.262
So I'd like to make two points.

00:47:12.262 --> 00:47:13.831 align:start size:81% position:19%
First of all, many
historians say

00:47:13.831 --> 00:47:16.066
that the reason they
bombed us to begin with

00:47:16.066 --> 00:47:20.003
was because of unfair tariffs
that we were in a terror for

00:47:20.003 --> 00:47:21.138
and secondly,

00:47:22.906 --> 00:47:26.443
they, you know, we
look at Japan now.

00:47:26.443 --> 00:47:29.446 align:start position:0%
I mean, look at all the Toyotas
and Hondas we buy from them

00:47:29.446 --> 00:47:31.081
they're not going
to bomb us today

00:47:31.081 --> 00:47:32.616 align:start size:81% position:19%
because you're
our best customer.

00:47:32.616 --> 00:47:34.918
You tend to not bomb
your best customer.

00:47:34.918 --> 00:47:36.653
So you say, you know,

00:47:36.653 --> 00:47:39.223
when you say foreign policy,

00:47:39.223 --> 00:47:41.191
I also include trade in that

00:47:41.191 --> 00:47:43.393
and when you look at
what Trump's doing

00:47:43.393 --> 00:47:46.830 align:start size:84% position:16%
basically having a
tariff war, with China

00:47:46.830 --> 00:47:49.933
I mean, you know, there's a
reason they use the term war.

00:47:49.933 --> 00:47:53.837
And I would say we need to
get out of any war with China

00:47:53.837 --> 00:47:58.041 align:start size:97% position:3%
that we, there's no way we
can get into a war with China.

00:47:58.041 --> 00:48:00.377
We have the ability to
obliterate each other.

00:48:00.377 --> 00:48:01.845
We cannot do that.

00:48:01.845 --> 00:48:05.048
And the best way to do
that is to open up trade.

00:48:05.048 --> 00:48:06.283
So just like, you know,

00:48:06.283 --> 00:48:08.819 align:start size:88% position:13%
we buy all these Toyotas
and Hondas from Japan

00:48:08.819 --> 00:48:10.053
and they're not gonna bomb us.

00:48:10.053 --> 00:48:11.855
How about we do the
same thing for China?

00:48:11.855 --> 00:48:13.090
Let's open up the borders.

00:48:13.090 --> 00:48:15.125
It's only hurting the citizens.

00:48:15.125 --> 00:48:16.727
And of course, I
know you've heard,

00:48:16.727 --> 00:48:18.962
you've probably said it where

00:48:18.962 --> 00:48:21.131
goods cross borders,
troops don't have to.

00:48:21.131 --> 00:48:23.166
So let's go by that model.

